## The idea

The k closest elements always form a **contiguous window** of length k in the sorted array. So we only need to find where the window **starts**, with binary search: compare x with the element just before the window's end and the one just after — if x is farther from arr[mid] than from arr[mid + k], the window must start further right. For counting distinct values in a huge sorted array, **jump over each run** of equal values with a binary search for where it ends, instead of reading every element.

## Step by step

1. KClosest: lo = 0, hi = n − k (the possible window starts).
2. While lo < hi: mid = (lo + hi) / 2. If x − arr[mid] > arr[mid + k] − x, the window starts after mid → lo = mid + 1; otherwise hi = mid.
3. Return arr[lo … lo + k − 1].
4. CountDistinct: start at index 0. Count 1, binary-search the last index holding the same value, jump to the index after it, repeat.

## Worked example

**Input:** arr = [1,2,3,4,5], k = 4, x = 3

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| range | window can start at 0 or 1 → lo 0, hi 1 |
| mid = 0 | x − arr[0] = 2; arr[4] − x = 2; 2 > 2 is false → hi = 0 |
| done | lo = 0 → window arr[0..3] |

**Result:** [1, 2, 3, 4]  (on a tie the smaller element wins)

## Cost

KClosest: O(log(n − k) + k). CountDistinct: O(d · log n) for d distinct values — far less than O(n) when n is huge and d is small. Memory O(1) beyond the output.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public static class SortedSearch {

    // The k elements closest to x, ascending; on a tie the smaller element wins.
    public static List<int> KClosest(int[] arr, int k, int x) {
        List<int> result = new List<int>();
        if (arr == null || k <= 0) return result;
        if (k > arr.Length) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(k));

        int lo = 0, hi = arr.Length - k;           // the answer is arr[lo .. lo + k - 1]
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            // long: the subtraction must not overflow for extreme values
            long distLeft = (long)x - arr[mid];
            long distRight = (long)arr[mid + k] - x;
            if (distLeft > distRight) lo = mid + 1;  // window must start further right
            else hi = mid;
        }

        for (int i = lo; i < lo + k; i++) result.Add(arr[i]);
        return result;
    }

    // Distinct values in a sorted array, jumping over each run of equal values.
    public static int CountDistinct(int[] a) {
        if (a == null || a.Length == 0) return 0;
        int count = 0;
        int i = 0;
        while (i < a.Length) {
            count++;
            int value = a[i];
            // binary search for the LAST index that still holds `value`
            int lo = i, hi = a.Length - 1;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;
                if (a[mid] == value) lo = mid;
                else hi = mid - 1;
            }
            i = lo + 1;                               // first index of the next value
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

